Autoimmune Anterior Uveitis (Mild Transient)
Autoimmune Conditions Mild Severity
Unilateral red eye, mild photophobia, associated with HLA-B27, resolves with topical drops

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: AMS-003
Reason for Visit: Acute unilateral painful red eye with photophobia requiring exclusion of sight-threatening pathology.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated urgent assessment was required to evaluate an acute red eye with visual disturbance. Prompt assessment was necessary to differentiate between benign conjunctivitis, acute angle-closure glaucoma, or infectious keratitis to prevent irreversible vision loss prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My left eye is completely red, aching, and the light is killing me.
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a 6-hour history of isolated, mild ocular inflammation in the left eye. Symptoms progressed from a gritty sensation to a dull, continuous ache accompanied by worsening photophobia. Patient denies trauma, chemical exposure, or purulent discharge. Reports a past history of lower back stiffness.
Functional Status: Independent, but currently keeping eyes closed due to light sensitivity.
Social/Safety Concerns: Low risk.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 128/82 | HR: 78 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 14 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 80 Kg
General: Alert, in mild discomfort, wearing sunglasses indoors.
Physical Exam: Left eye exhibits circumcorneal ciliary flush. Unilateral red eye, mild photophobia, associated with HLA-B27, resolves with topical drops. Pupils are equal and reactive, though the left pupil is slightly sluggish and miotic. Visual acuity is grossly intact. Right eye is quiet and normal. No purulent discharge. Intraocular pressure palpates symmetrically normal.
Environmental Observations: House lights dimmed, no trip hazards identified, environment safe.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. No relevant current medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Autoimmune Anterior Uveitis (Mild Transient) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Angle-Closure Glaucoma - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Normal palpated intraocular pressure, absence of fixed mid-dilated pupil, no nausea or severe headache.

Infectious Keratitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of corneal opacity, ulceration, or purulent discharge.

Viral Conjunctivitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Presence of severe photophobia, ciliary flush pattern, and absence of watery discharge or contralateral eye involvement.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute onset unilateral ocular pain and photophobia. Triage correctly mandated urgent evaluation to rule out acute angle-closure glaucoma and infectious keratitis, both of which can cause permanent visual impairment if untreated overnight. Clinical examination confirmed characteristic signs of anterior uveitis without elevated intraocular pressure or corneal defect. Timely assessment permitted the safe initiation of symptomatic management and referral planning, which could not have been delayed until the next in-hours period without risking severe pain escalation and potential structural eye damage (synechiae).

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered topical cycloplegic drop for pain relief. Urgent referral generated for ophthalmology review in the AM for topical corticosteroid initiation.
Positive Behavioral Change: Wear dark sunglasses indoors and apply cold compresses for 10 minutes every hour. Engaging in active light mitigation will rapidly decrease ciliary muscle spasm pain, reinforcing compliance with ocular rest.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None indicated.
Follow-up: Urgent optometry or ophthalmology review within 12-24 hours.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If you experience sudden severe worsening of eye pain, sudden loss of vision, vomiting, or extreme headache, you must present to the nearest Emergency Department immediately.

